<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    A work mech waiting at length instead of asking for work again straight away.

    What this is for. A work mech with nothing to do does not stand still today: the bottom of
    RimWorld's Mechanoid think tree hands an idle player mech JobGiver_WanderColony, so it takes a
    short walk, the walk ends, and the whole tree runs from the top again including JobGiver_Work
    twice, once in emergency mode and once not. Each of those is a full pass over every work giver
    the mech has. This replaces the wander with one long wait, which cuts how often that happens and
    stops the milling about.

    Deliberately NOT JobDefOf.SelfShutdown. That is the obvious candidate and it is wrong:
    Need_MechEnergy.IsSelfShutdown tests CurJobDef == JobDefOf.SelfShutdown and a mech in that state
    gains BaseGainPerDaySelfShutdown energy. Hibernation is a performance setting and must not hand
    out free charge. On this def the mech stays on the ordinary idle fall rate of 3 per day, which is
    exactly what it pays for standing around now.

    driverClass is vanilla's own JobDriver_Wait, unmodified. It branches on the job def in only one
    place, for Wait_Combat, so ours behaves as a plain wait; and it runs CheckForAutoAttack every
    tick, which is why a hibernating mech still shoots at anything that walks up to it.

    isIdle is true so the game counts this as idle time rather than as work, which keeps the idle
    energy fall rate and keeps a hibernating mech out of the "doing something" reckoning.

    suspendable false matches vanilla's Wait: there is nothing to resume, and a suspended wait would
    only be restarted from the top anyway.

    No expiryInterval here. The giver sets it per job, because the length is the setting's whole
    point and belongs where it can be read rather than in a def a player would have to decompile.

    Referenced from C# as MechDefOf.Gideon_MechHibernate.
  -->
  <JobDef>
    <defName>Gideon_MechHibernate</defName>
    <driverClass>JobDriver_Wait</driverClass>
    <reportString>hibernating.</reportString>
    <isIdle>true</isIdle>
    <suspendable>false</suspendable>
    <carryThingAfterJob>true</carryThingAfterJob>
    <dropThingBeforeJob>false</dropThingBeforeJob>
  </JobDef>

</Defs>
